      "id": 1,
      "text": "Willis began as the village of Hylton in the late 19th century before being renamed after the Willis family who ran a local general store.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/willis_floyd_county_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The post office started as Greasy Creek in 1840, moved to Hylton in 1880, and finally became Willis in 1894.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        4,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/willis_floyd_county_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A Lutheran Church built in 1879 with a white spire became a prominent landmark above the hilltops.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/willis_floyd_county_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Willis sits at 2,723 feet in the Blue Ridge Mountains, giving it cooler summers and snowy winters.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        21,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/willis_floyd_county_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Mountain Normal School opened in 1893, later evolving into Willis Agricultural High School, a center for teacher training.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/willis_floyd_county_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Dense woodlands around Willis host wildlife such as deer, turkey, and songbirds, reflecting the rich biodiversity of the Blue Ridge.",
